// u6 Contracts and Sales (MBE): Formation, Defenses, Content and meaning, Performance/breach/discharge, Remedies, Third-party rights.
// Written from research/contracts.md, CURRICULUM.md §7 u6 and questions.md §9. Every fact pattern is our own; no NCBE or commercial-outline text.
// First move in every hypo: goods (UCC Article 2) or not (common law)? The stem says which in its first sentences, and states any split rule.
// Provenance: UCC section text read on LII is `book`; Restatement (Second) doctrine and case holdings are `general`.
//
// UNSURE items (excluded from the Question of the Day and the mocks; a second reader must check each before it ships):
//  - u6-r-207-different and u6-h-knockout-delivery: the majority "knockout" rule for different terms in 2-207. Official Comment 6 and the
//    leading cases (Daitom v. Pennwalt and others) were read only in secondary sources; NCBE may flag it as a testable split. The stem names the rule.
//  - u6-h-willful-wiring: whether a willful breacher may rely on substantial performance varies by state (contracts.md §21 C6-07 [U]);
//    the stem states the jurisdiction's rule and the key (restitution net of damages, Rest. (2d) §374) should be re-read.
// Not taught: the 2022-only wording of the hybrid-transaction amendments to UCC 2-102/2-106 (the predominant-purpose test, which both texts share,
// is taught and drilled: u6-h-hybrid-security, u6-h-firm-offer-hybrid) and the 2003 Article 2 amendments (withdrawn).
// NextGen overlay covered here: hybrid transactions, E-SIGN/UETA (u6-r-201, u6-h-email-signature), force majeure (u6-h-force-majeure),
// employment-contract breach (u6-h-mitigation-manager, u6-r-good-faith), retraction of repudiation (u6-h-retraction-caterer),
// beneficiary defenses (u6-h-tpb-defense), anti-assignment clauses (u6-h-anti-assignment), restitution for a breaching party (u6-h-breaching-contractor).
// Items resting on a split state the approach in the stem: u6-h-past-help (traditional past-consideration rule), u6-h-garage-modification
// (pre-existing duty, no section 89), u6-h-guardianship-car (guardianship void), u6-h-noncompete (modern blue-pencil approach), u6-h-land-deposit
// (part performance needs possession plus payment or improvements), u6-h-vesting-son (Restatement (Second) 311; labelled "modern" versus "traditional",
// not majority and minority), u6-h-assignment-successive (first-in-time). A reviewer should confirm those stems against NCBE's current wording of each split,
// and in particular the "majority" label on first-in-time in u6-r-assignment, which is general knowledge, not read in a sourcebook.
// Official Comments to the UCC are not on LII and were read only in secondary sources: every item that rests on one (u6-r-merchant, u6-r-modification,
// u6-st-207, u6-r-207-additional, u6-so-material-alteration, u6-h-207-arbitration, u6-h-steel-cost, u6-h-steel-embargo) is `general`, not `book`.
// u6-r-merchant / 2-104 Comment 2: merchant status for 2-314 versus the business-practice sections comes from secondary sources (LII has no comments).
// Good faith: the revised Article 1 definition (1-201(b)(20), no merchant limit) is taught as the default; the older 2-103(1)(b) merchant-only text
// is a `split` the stem must name.
